<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1919</h1><p class="ref-lede">1919 in world history: 38 events, 22 births, 24 deaths, 4 works, 24 states founded and 25 that came to an end. 756 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a><a href="/years/1916">1916</a><a href="/years/1917">1917</a><a href="/years/1918">1918</a><a href="/years/1919" aria-current="page">1919</a><a href="/years/1920">1920</a><a href="/years/1921">1921</a><a href="/years/1922">1922</a><a href="/years/1923">1923</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">German Workers' Party founded in Munich</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Railway locksmith Anton Drexler founded the small nationalist German Workers' Party. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> joined that autumn and in 1920 the party was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">National Socialist German Workers' Party</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht murdered in Berlin</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a>, the two communist leaders were seized and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> soldiers. The murders deepened the bitter split on the German left.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-16">16 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ratification of the Eighteenth Amendment sets up Prohibition</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Nebraska became the 36th state to ratify the amendment banning the manufacture and sale of alcohol, making it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">US Constitution</a>. Prohibition took effect a year later and lasted until 1933.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-31">31 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Police clash with strikers in Glasgow's George Square</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A rally for a 40-hour week ended in violence between police and workers in George Square. Fearing revolution, the government sent troops and tanks into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-26">26 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Grand Canyon becomes a US national park</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> signed the act creating Grand Canyon National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>. The gorge, carved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> River, is over 1.6 km deep in places.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Koreans rise against Japanese rule in the March 1st Movement</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A declaration of independence was read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, and peaceful demonstrations spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Japanese forces suppressed them, killing thousands, but the movement inspired Korean nationalism.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-23">23 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Mussolini founds the Fasci di Combattimento in Milan</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> gathered veterans and nationalists in Piazza San Sepolcro to found a new movement. Its blackshirts helped him take power in 1922, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> became a model for Hitler.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amritsar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Troops fire on a crowd at Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Soldiers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginald-dyer">Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer</a> fired without warning into an unarmed crowd in an enclosed garden. At least 379 were killed by official count; the massacre hardened Indian opposition to British rule.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-28">28 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Versailles is signed</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> signed the peace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a>, five years to the day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>. It accepted territorial losses, disarmament and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, which fuelled lasting resentment.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">President Ebert signs the Weimar Constitution</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> signed the constitution of the new German republic, with universal suffrage and a strong president. Its emergency powers were later exploited in Hitler's rise to power.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First daily international scheduled air service begins</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Aircraft Transport and Travel began daily flights between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s Hounslow Heath and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, carrying passengers, mail and goods. It was the start of scheduled international commercial aviation.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye (1919)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye was the peace treaty signed on 10 September 1919 between the Allied powers and the new Republic of Austria after the First World War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Jallianwala Bagh massacre took place on 13 April 1919 in Amritsar, Punjab, when British Indian troops under Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer fired without warning on a large unarmed crowd in an enclosed garden.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cesis">Battle of Cēsis</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Cēsis was fought in June 1919 around the town of Cēsis in northern Latvia.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-first-movement.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolt</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The March First Movement was a nationwide Korean protest against Japanese colonial rule that began on 1 March 1919 with the reading of a Declaration of Independence in Seoul.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/vix-note" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vix-note.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/vix-note">Vix Note</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Vix Note was an Allied demand handed to the Hungarian government on 20 March 1919 by the French lieutenant colonel Fernand Vix.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-uprising" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-uprising.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolt</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/christmas-uprising">Christmas Uprising</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Christmas Uprising was a revolt in Montenegro in early January 1919, around Orthodox Christmas, by supporters of independence and of the deposed King Nicholas I, known as the Greens.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/polish-czechoslovak-war">Polish–Czechoslovak War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Polish–Czechoslovak War, also called the Seven-Day War, was a brief conflict in late January 1919 over Cieszyn Silesia.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/red-terror-hungary">Red Terror (Hungary)</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Red Terror in Hungary was the campaign of repression carried out by the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 against its real and supposed enemies.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolt</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Spartacist uprising was a general strike and armed rising in Berlin from 5 to 12 January 1919, led by the newly founded Communist Party of Germany and other radicals against the Social Democratic government of Friedrich Ebert.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Third Anglo-Afghan War was a brief conflict in May 1919, when Emir Amanullah Khan of Afghanistan sent troops across the frontier of British India.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuilly-sur-seine" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuilly-sur-seine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuilly-sur-seine">Treaty of Neuilly-sur-Seine</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Neuilly-sur-Seine was the peace treaty between the Allied powers and Bulgaria after the First World War, signed on 27 November 1919.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-radviliskis">Battle of Radviliškis</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Radviliškis was fought in November 1919 around the railway town of Radviliškis in northern Lithuania, where Lithuanian forces defeated the West Russian Volunteer Army of Pavel Bermondt-Avalov.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-agreement">Anglo-Persian Agreement</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Anglo-Persian Agreement was a treaty negotiated in August 1919 between Britain, led by Lord Curzon, and the government of Vosuq od-Dowleh in Qajar Iran.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-peregonovka">Battle of Peregonovka</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Peregonovka was fought in September 1919 near the village of Peregonovka (Perehonivka), near Uman in central Ukraine, during the Russian Civil War.</p></div></li></ol></section>
